"mo\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"><use data-c=\"2212\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2212\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(633,-247) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"37\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-37\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3234.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> : In these cases, chromium exhibits an oxidation state of +6.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Hence, the common positive oxidation states for Chromium are +2, +3, and +6. <br><br>Therefore, the correct option is :</p>\n<p>Option B : +2 to +6.</p>"
